---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import SolarSim from '../../simulators/solar/SolarSim.astro';
import { solarQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/solar';

const toc = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'What is the solar system?' },
  { id: 'planets', label: 'The eight planets' },
  { id: 'scale', label: 'A sense of scale' },
  { id: 'orbits', label: 'How the planets move' },
  { id: 'formation', label: 'How it formed' },
  { id: 'more', label: 'Beyond the planets' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Williams, D. R. Planetary Fact Sheet – Metric. NASA Goddard Space Flight Center.', url: 'https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/' },
  { text: 'NASA Science. Solar System Exploration: Our Solar System.', url: 'https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/' },
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Chapter 7: Other Worlds: An Introduction to the Solar System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/7-introduction' },
  { text: 'Simon, J. L., et al. (1994). Numerical expressions for precession formulae and mean elements for the Moon and the planets. Astronomy and Astrophysics, 282, 663–683.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="astronomy"
  topic="solar-system"
  description="Interactive 3D solar system: watch the planets orbit from today’s date, switch to true scale, compare planet sizes and explore facts about each world."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={solarQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Eight planets, hundreds of moons and countless smaller bodies circle our Sun. Explore them in 3D, starting from
      where they are today, speed up time to watch them orbit, and switch to true scale to discover just how vast and
      empty the solar system really is.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <SolarSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Drag</strong> to rotate the view and scroll or pinch to zoom (on touch screens, tap the view first).</li>
      <li><strong>Click a planet</strong> or the Sun to see its facts in the readouts.</li>
      <li>Change <strong>Time speed</strong> and watch the inner planets race round while the outer planets crawl. <strong>Jump to today</strong> resets the date.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Follow a planet</strong> to ride along with one world.</li>
      <li>Switch the <strong>View</strong> to <strong>True scale</strong> (zoom right in to find the planets) and to <strong>Compare sizes</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="overview">What is the solar system?</h2>
  <p>
    The solar system is the Sun and everything held in orbit by its gravity: eight planets and their moons, dwarf planets
    such as Pluto, asteroids, comets and dust. The Sun contains about 99.8 % of all the mass, which is why everything
    else orbits it. The planets all travel in the same direction and in nearly the same flat plane, a clue to how the
    solar system formed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="planets">The eight planets</h2>
  <p>
    The four <strong>inner, rocky planets</strong> are Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. They are small and dense, with solid
    surfaces. The four <strong>outer giant planets</strong> are Jupiter and Saturn, the <strong>gas giants</strong> made
    mostly of hydrogen and helium, and Uranus and Neptune, the <strong>ice giants</strong> with large amounts of water,
    ammonia and methane ices. All four giants have rings and many moons. The asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter
    separates the two groups.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Planet</th><th>Distance (AU)</th><th>Diameter (Earth = 1)</th><th>Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>0.39</td><td>0.38</td><td>88 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Venus</td><td>0.72</td><td>0.95</td><td>225 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Earth</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>365 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mars</td><td>1.52</td><td>0.53</td><td>1.9 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>5.20</td><td>11.2</td><td>11.9 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Saturn</td><td>9.54</td><td>9.4</td><td>29.4 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Uranus</td><td>19.2</td><td>4.0</td><td>84 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Neptune</td><td>30.1</td><td>3.9</td><td>165 years</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="scale">A sense of scale</h2>
  <p>
    Most pictures of the solar system, including the “Fit everything” view here, are not to scale, because a true-scale
    picture would show almost nothing. If the Sun were the size of a football (22 cm), Earth would be a peppercorn about
    24 m away, Jupiter a large marble about 120 m away, and Neptune a small bead nearly 700 m away. Light, the fastest
    thing in the universe, takes about 8 minutes to reach us from the Sun and over 4 hours to reach Neptune.
  </p>

  <h2 id="orbits">How the planets move</h2>
  <p>
    Gravity from the Sun pulls each planet towards it, while the planet’s sideways speed carries it forward, so it falls
    around the Sun in an <strong>orbit</strong>. Planets closer to the Sun feel stronger gravity and move faster. Johannes
    Kepler discovered that the square of a planet’s orbital period is proportional to the cube of its distance
    (<strong>Kepler’s third law</strong>): Jupiter is about 5.2 times further from the Sun than Earth, and
    5.2³ ≈ 141 ≈ 11.9², so its year is about 11.9 Earth years. Real orbits are slightly stretched ellipses; this model
    uses circles, which is a good approximation for most planets.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formation">How it formed</h2>
  <p>
    About 4.6 billion years ago a cloud of gas and dust collapsed under its own gravity. Most of the material fell to the
    centre and became the Sun, while the rest flattened into a spinning disc. Near the hot young Sun only rock and metal
    could stay solid, forming small rocky planets. Further out, beyond the “frost line”, ices could also form, so planets
    grew big enough to pull in huge amounts of hydrogen and helium gas.
  </p>

  <h2 id="more">Beyond the planets</h2>
  <p>
    Past Neptune lies the <strong>Kuiper Belt</strong>, home to icy dwarf planets such as Pluto and Eris. Much further out,
    the <strong>Oort Cloud</strong> is thought to surround the solar system in a giant shell of icy bodies and is the source
    of long-period comets. Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006 because it has not cleared its orbit of other
    objects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Astronomical unit (AU)</dt>
    <dd>The average Earth–Sun distance, about 149.6 million km.</dd>
    <dt>Orbital period</dt>
    <dd>The time for one complete orbit: a planet’s year.</dd>
    <dt>Dwarf planet</dt>
    <dd>A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit of other objects.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The planets line up neatly in a row.”</strong> They are spread around their orbits; true alignments are rare.</li>
    <li><strong>“Space between the planets is small.”</strong> The planets would fit between Earth and the Moon several times over.</li>
    <li><strong>“Summer happens when Earth is closest to the Sun.”</strong> Seasons are caused by Earth’s tilt; Earth is actually closest in January.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Sun holds almost all the mass, and its gravity keeps the planets in orbit.</li>
    <li>Four small rocky inner planets, four giant outer planets.</li>
    <li>Further planets orbit more slowly and have much longer years (Kepler’s third law).</li>
    <li>The solar system is enormous and mostly empty space.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: orbits are drawn as circles in a single plane. Positions start from today’s date using each planet’s average position, so they can differ from the real sky by a few degrees. Moon counts keep rising as new small moons are discovered.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
